# oauthindie — IndieAuth client for DokuWiki

`oauthindie` adds an IndieAuth identity login to DokuWiki's `oauth` authentication plugin. Users enter their website or Misskey instance hostname. The plugin discovers server endpoints, performs the IndieAuth authorization-code flow with PKCE S256, then fetches the authenticated Misskey account.

## Endpoint and client discovery

- For Misskey, the plugin first fetches `https://<instance>/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server` and reads `authorization_endpoint` and `token_endpoint` from the JSON metadata. This is server endpoint discovery; it is separate from client metadata discovery.
- If server metadata is unavailable, the plugin falls back to IndieAuth `authorization_endpoint` and `token_endpoint` links in the identity page's HTML or HTTP `Link` headers.
- Misskey also discovers the DokuWiki client at the `client_id` URL. The OAuth adapter adds `<link rel="redirect_uri" href="...">` to DokuWiki's page head, using the OAuth plugin's configured `redirectURI()` (normally `DOKU_URL . DOKU_SCRIPT`). The advertised URI must exactly match the `redirect_uri` in the authorization request. This follows the HTML client discovery path supported by Misskey; Misskey v2026.3.0+ also supports JSON client metadata served from the client ID URL.

## Login and account mapping

1. Enter a hostname such as `misskey.example` or a full identity URL. Hostnames are normalized to HTTPS.
2. The plugin requests the `read:account` scope by default and includes a random state value and PKCE S256 challenge.
3. On callback, it exchanges the code with the PKCE verifier and verifies the returned `me` identity.
4. It calls Misskey's authenticated account API (`POST /api/i`). The account's `username` and `host` become the DokuWiki login identifier and synthetic email (`username@host`); the account `name` becomes the display name. If `host` is null for a local account, the instance host is used. The synthetic address is an identifier, not a deliverable email.

Endpoint discovery only accepts public HTTPS URLs and does not follow redirects. Discovery requests to a user-provided hostname are server-side, so private and reserved addresses are rejected.

## Setup

- Install under `lib/plugins/oauthindie/`.
- Install and enable DokuWiki's `oauth` plugin as the authentication backend.
- Ensure DokuWiki's canonical site URL and OAuth plugin redirect URI are correct and accessible over HTTPS.
- The DokuWiki page at the configured client ID URL must expose the exact callback `redirect_uri`; the plugin adds a `rel="redirect_uri"` link to the page head dynamically.

See the [Misskey OAuth documentation](https://misskey-hub.net/en/docs/for-developers/api/token/oauth/), [Misskey PR #17030](https://github.com/misskey-dev/misskey/pull/17030), and the [IndieAuth specification](https://indieauth.spec.indieweb.org/).
